.TH EUCLID-CLI-EES-UNSUBSCRIBE-EVENTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ees-unsubscribe-events \- remove a subscription and the events waiting for it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ees unsubscribe-events
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-event\-type\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fITYPE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Removes a subscription, and every event still waiting for it.
.PP
The events go with it deliberately. Keeping a backlog for something
nobody is listening to any more is what the seven-day retention exists
to prevent, and unsubscribing says that in so many words \- so it is
done now rather than a week from now.
.PP
Naming no event type removes the subscriber entirely, which is what
decommissioning an application wants. Naming one narrows the subscriber
and leaves its other subscriptions, and their backlogs, alone.
.PP
Note that this is not how to pause a consumer: a subscription that is
removed and re-created misses everything published in between, because
between the two there was nothing to store the events for. To stop
processing for a while, simply stop receiving \- the events wait.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Subscriber name. Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-event\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Event type to stop receiving. Omitted removes all of this subscriber's
subscriptions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call succeeded. The number of subscriptions removed is printed as
JSON; removing something that was not subscribed reports zero rather
than failing.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no session, or the server rejected the
request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stop watching deletions, keep watching uploads:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees unsubscribe-events \e
    --name invoice-import --event-type esm.object.deleted
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decommission a consumer entirely:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees unsubscribe-events --name invoice-import
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-subscribe-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-list-subscriptions (1)
